WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.740
Anano-scale platform now tracks single proteins moving inside living neurons in real-time. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 219. RNA polymerase selection comes next, then isomer mapping on surfaces, with a science deep dive on how copying accuracy actually evolves. The University of Queensland built NASTIC to watch disease-related proteins one molecule at a time inside living cells, according to Fizz.org. The platform logs where each protein sits and when each

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:31.120
molecular event happens.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:36.860
The work has been spun out into a Cretian bio, a Brisbane startup for early Alzheimer's

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.400
and Parkinson's drug discovery.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.380
It runs in intact neurons, not in cells that have been fixed.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:47.300
That gives a direct look at how given proteins act in healthy states and in disease.

00:00:49.151 --> 00:00:53.911
Older approaches either froze the cells in place or average signals across many molecules,

00:00:54.451 --> 00:00:56.071
so the order of events disappeared.

00:00:56.071 --> 00:01:01.671
The live single molecule record can show how specific proteins cluster or disperse during

00:01:01.671 --> 00:01:05.391
ordinary function and during early signs of neurodegeneration.

00:01:05.931 --> 00:01:10.491
Drug developers can test whether candidate compounds alter those patterns before visible

00:01:10.491 --> 00:01:11.591
cell damage occurs.

00:01:12.331 --> 00:01:14.891
The same platform works across different cell types.

00:01:15.451 --> 00:01:20.171
That comparison can ask whether the same proteins behave differently in neurons versus glia.

00:01:21.204 --> 00:01:28.864
An RNA polymerase ribosim now copies functional RNAs with 94% fidelity per nucleotide, according

00:01:28.864 --> 00:01:35.964
to a BioRXIV preprint. 33 rounds of selection produced that enzyme. The gain came from fewer

00:01:35.964 --> 00:01:41.204
mismatched incorporations and from fewer extensions of partial products that ended in mismatches.

00:01:41.724 --> 00:01:46.924
The enzyme also copies a wider range of templates, including highly structured sequences that

00:01:46.924 --> 00:01:53.124
earlier versions handled poorly. When tested on the Class I ligase ribosim, the new polymerase

00:01:53.124 --> 00:01:58.504
produced full-length functional copies more reliably. The work targets two long-standing limits

00:01:58.504 --> 00:02:04.824
on using RNA polymerases to replicate and evolve other functional RNAs, copying accuracy and

00:02:04.824 --> 00:02:11.148
template sequence generality. Something most people get wrong is the belief that an RNA copying

00:02:11.148 --> 00:02:16.488
enzyme is stuck with whatever accuracy its first fold allows, as if that number were sealed

00:02:16.488 --> 00:02:22.388
unless someone redesigned the molecule from the outside. Laboratory selection can reshape how an RNA

00:02:22.388 --> 00:02:27.168
polymerase ribosome treats both the right nucleotide and the wrong one as a chain grows.

00:02:27.888 --> 00:02:32.428
The process starts with a mixed population of ribosomes that differ slightly in sequence.

00:02:33.128 --> 00:02:38.248
Each round puts that population under conditions that reward accurate copying of functional RNA

00:02:38.248 --> 00:02:43.728
templates. Variants that copy less accurately are discarded. Over successive rounds,

00:02:43.728 --> 00:02:48.268
the population converges on sequences that insert fewer mismatched nucleotides.

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:52.768
Those sequences also stop extending when a mismatch sits at the growing end,

00:02:53.308 --> 00:02:54.888
instead of pushing past the error.

00:02:55.488 --> 00:03:00.048
The enzyme that emerges keeps higher overall fidelity across long RNA products.

00:03:00.728 --> 00:03:05.628
Inside living systems, the same principle operates whenever natural selection acts on replication

00:03:05.628 --> 00:03:11.028
machinery. Variants that reduce error rates gain an advantage when accurate transmission of

00:03:11.028 --> 00:03:12.928
genetic information improves survival.

00:03:13.528 --> 00:03:15.608
The laboratory version shows that fidelity

00:03:15.608 --> 00:03:17.028
is not an inherent ceiling.

00:03:17.608 --> 00:03:20.008
It is an outcome of the selective pressures applied

00:03:20.008 --> 00:03:20.928
during evolution.

00:03:21.688 --> 00:03:23.408
Further changes in selection conditions,

00:03:23.768 --> 00:03:25.128
such as different template links

00:03:25.128 --> 00:03:26.908
or added chemical modifications,

00:03:27.408 --> 00:03:28.768
could drive additional gains.

00:03:29.548 --> 00:03:32.108
Accuracy remains adjustable rather than locked.

00:03:32.748 --> 00:03:35.368
Future polymerase designs can be tuned systematically

00:03:35.368 --> 00:03:37.208
rather than discovered by chance.

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:45.058
Scientists at NPL, the University of Surrey, the University of Oxford, the Francis Crick

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:51.518
Institute, and IONTOFGMBH created a technique that maps molecules sharing the same chemical

00:03:51.518 --> 00:03:54.438
formula, yet differing in three-dimensional arrangement.

00:03:55.078 --> 00:03:59.598
The method distinguishes islmers that have the same mass but different structures and functions,

00:04:00.098 --> 00:04:02.138
as reported today in nature communications.

00:04:02.778 --> 00:04:03.918
It works on surfaces.

00:04:03.918 --> 00:04:07.778
Spatial information is preserved while those molecules are mapped.

00:04:08.438 --> 00:04:12.378
Molecules that look identical by mass alone can now be told apart in place.

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:22.690
University of Javascular chemists developed a strategy that direct synthetic molecules to self-assemble into defined three-dimensional triple helices.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:28.910
Fizz.org reports that the method gives control over the final architecture that earlier approaches lacked.

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:34.650
Act. Artificial systems move closer to the precise organization seen in natural macromolecules.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:38.890
The study brings that level of order into molecules built from scratch.

00:04:41.079 --> 00:04:45.959
Honeybeehives maintain multiple antimicrobial defenses that protect the colony, its

00:04:45.959 --> 00:04:48.979
brood, and stored food, according to Fizz.org.

00:04:49.419 --> 00:04:52.419
The layered system reduces disease spread inside the hive.

00:04:52.979 --> 00:04:57.919
Those defenses act as a living immune system for the colony, its offspring, and its food

00:04:57.919 --> 00:04:58.379
supplies.

00:04:58.379 --> 00:05:02.839
Protecting B habitat is tied to keeping that layered defense intact.

00:05:04.209 --> 00:05:09.009
Birds transport nutrients from land, lakes, and sea through waste and carcasses,

00:05:09.429 --> 00:05:13.469
according to a Fizz.org report on a review of nearly 400 studies.

00:05:13.909 --> 00:05:17.949
Researchers from Cornell and five partner institutions compiled the synthesis.

00:05:18.709 --> 00:05:22.949
The movement alters soil chemistry, water chemistry, plant growth, and animal

00:05:22.949 --> 00:05:27.869
communities far from nesting sites. The effect spans coral reefs, forests, and

00:05:27.869 --> 00:05:33.329
open ocean. Birds distribute nutrients at large scale and sustain life well beyond the nest.

00:05:35.637 --> 00:05:40.897
John Innis Center researchers train scientists from across Tanzania to use loop mediated

00:05:40.897 --> 00:05:47.657
isothermal amplification or lamp assays according to Fizz.org. The tool rapidly detects wheat

00:05:47.657 --> 00:05:53.377
blast fungus. Wheat blast is a looming fungal threat to wheat harvest in the region. Training

00:05:53.377 --> 00:05:56.097
The training covered both field and laboratory use of the assays.

00:05:57.384 --> 00:06:02.244
Full implementation of current national methane plans could reduce global emissions by

00:06:02.244 --> 00:06:09.624
11% between 2020 and 2030, according to an IIASA-led analysis reported by FIS.org.

00:06:10.364 --> 00:06:12.584
UNEP colleagues co-authored the study.

00:06:13.264 --> 00:06:16.004
The work combined government pledges with emissions modeling.

00:06:16.624 --> 00:06:19.084
That path would reverse the recent rise in emissions.

00:06:19.084 --> 00:06:23.224
The reduction still falls short of the global methane pledge target.

00:06:24.868 --> 00:06:29.168
William and Mary's Virginia Institute of Marine Science recorded average numbers of young

00:06:29.168 --> 00:06:34.368
of year striped bass in Chesapeake Bay tributaries this spring, according to Fizz.org.

00:06:34.888 --> 00:06:39.328
Preliminary results point to an average year class produced in the Virginia tributaries

00:06:39.328 --> 00:06:40.368
in 2026.

00:06:40.928 --> 00:06:45.008
Fish hatched in 2026 will reach fishable size in three to four years.

00:06:45.008 --> 00:06:48.588
Stripe Bass serve as top predators in the Bay ecosystem.

00:06:48.888 --> 00:06:51.988
They support both commercial and recreational fisheries.

00:06:52.548 --> 00:06:56.568
The ongoing long-term survey supplies the data used for management decisions.

00:06:58.339 --> 00:07:04.239
A temporary expansion of the earned income tax credit, also today, reduced housing insecurity

00:07:04.239 --> 00:07:07.719
among young workers without children, according to Fizz.org.

00:07:08.299 --> 00:07:13.939
New mapping identified 13% of Earth's land as biodiversity conservation hotspots ahead

00:07:13.939 --> 00:07:16.499
of the UN biodiversity conference in Armenia.

00:07:17.199 --> 00:07:22.519
Before we go, the next concrete step for the improved RNA polymerase ribosim is testing

00:07:22.519 --> 00:07:27.559
whether it holds fidelity on still longer functional RNAs and inside model cells.

